degradability.\ndf['polar_to_heavy_atom_ratio'] = df['SpPosA_B(p)'] / (df['nHM'] + 1e-6)  # Adding 1e-6 to avoid division by zero\n\n# oxygen_to_heavy_atom_ratio\n# Feature description: Ratio of nO (number of oxygen atoms) to nHM (number of heavy atoms).\n# Usefulness: Oxygen's presence relative to heavy atoms may influence the molecule's probability of being biologically degraded.\ndf['oxygen_to_heavy_atom_ratio'] = df['nO'] / (df['nHM'] + 1e-6)  # Adding 1e-6 to avoid division by zero\n```end\n\n### Step 3: Interaction Terms for Capturing Relationships\nCreate new features by combining existing attributes to account for non-linear interactions.\n\n```python\n# interaction_SM6_L_and_TI2_L\n# Feature description: Multiplication of SM6_L (spectral moment of order 6 from Laplace matrix) and TI2_L (second Mohar index from Laplace matrix).\n# Usefulness: Encodes higher-order interactions between molecular connectivity and structural complexity, which could be predictive of biodegradability.\ndf['interaction_SM6_L_and_TI2_L'] = df['SM6_L'] * df['TI2_L']\n\n# interaction_HyWi_B(m)_and_nO\n# Feature description: Multiplication of HyWi_B(m) (Hyper-Wiener-like index) and nO (number of oxygen atoms).\n# Usefulness: Enhances the importance of molecules with high mass-related indices and oxygen content, key factors for biodegradation prediction.\ndf['interaction_HyWi_B(m)_and_nO'] = df['HyWi_B(m)'] * df['nO']\n\n# interaction_C_percent_and_SdO\n# Feature description: Multiplication of C% (percentage of carbon atoms) and SdO (sum of dO E-states).\n# Usefulness: Encodes relationships between carbon composition and electronic state contributions, potentially indicating molecular reactivity.\ndf['interaction_C_percent_and_SdO'] = df['C%'] * df['SdO']\n```end\n\n### Dropping Low-Variance Columns\nFeatures with little variation provide limited information and add noise to the model.\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column B03[C-Cl] is dropped\n# The column B03[C-Cl] is binary, with most values being zero (mean = 0.1343, std = 0.3412), offering minimal predictive value.\ndf.drop(columns=['B03[C-Cl']', inplace=True)\n\n# Explanation why the column B01[C-Br] is dropped\n# The column B01[C-Br] is highly sparse, with most values being zero (mean = 0.0442, std = 0.2058), indicating it adds little insight.\ndf.drop(columns=['B01[C-Br]'], inplace=True)\n\n# Explanation why the column nN-N is dropped\n# The column nN-N has an almost negligible mean (0.0111) and standard deviation (0.1188), showing low variability and redundancy.\ndf.drop(columns=['nN-N'], inplace=True)\n```end ", "Here are the three next steps for feature engineering, aiming to improve the downstream prediction task of classifying biodegradability. Each feature is designed to appropriately reflect real-world molecular interactions, enhance model interpretability, and address potential issues like skewness or lack of interaction modeling.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Log Transformation for Highly Skewed Features\nSeveral features exhibit large ranges and significant skewness. Applying a logarithmic transformation can normalize their distribution and reduce the effect of extreme values, making them more informative for machine learning models.\n\n```python\n# log_F03[C-O]\n# Feature description: Logarithm of the frequency of C-O bonds at topological distance 3 (F03[C-O]).\n# Usefulness: As F03[C-O] (mean = 3.6524, median = 2.0) is positively skewed, this transformation reduces skewness and improves feature variability for the model.\ndf['log_F03[C-O]'] = np.log1p(df['F03[C-O]'])\n\n# log_F04[C-N]\n# Feature description: Logarithm of the frequency of C-N bonds at topological distance 4 (F04[C-N]).\n# Usefulness: This feature is highly skewed (mean = 0.9637, median = 0.0), and the log transformation avoids dampening outliers while making the data distribution more normal.\ndf['log_F04[C-N]'] = np.log1p(df['F04[C-N]'])\n\n# log_SdO\n# Feature description: Logarithm of the sum of dO E-states (SdO