###["Here are three possible next steps to generate features for improving the downstream task of biodegradability prediction:\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Transform highly skewed features with logarithmic scaling\nSome features such as F04[C-N], F03[C-O], F02[C-N], and nCIR are heavily skewed (as indicated by large ranges and means much greater than medians). Applying a logarithmic transformation can help normalize their distributions, which may improve the model's ability to learn patterns.\n\npython\n# log_F04[C-N]\n# Logarithmic transformation of F04[C-N], which represents the frequency of C-N bonds at topological distance 4\n# Usefulness: Reducing skewness in this feature increases its predictive power, as extreme values are rescaled to more manageable ranges.\ndf['log_F04[C-N]'] = df['F04[C-N]'].apply(lambda x: np.log1p(x))\n\n# log_F03[C-O]\n# Logarithmic transformation of F03[C-O], which represents the frequency of C-O bonds at topological distance 3\n# Usefulness: This transformation creates a more balanced input distribution and reduces the effect of extreme values, potentially making the feature more useful for the model.\ndf['log_F03[C-O]'] = df['F03[C-O]'].apply(lambda x: np.log1p(x))\n\n# log_F02[C-N]\n# Logarithmic transformation of F02[C-N], representing frequency of C-N bonds at topological distance 2\n# Usefulness: Similar to the above, normalizing its range enables a better capture of relationships in the model.\ndf['log_F02[C-N]'] = df['F02[C-N]'].apply(lambda x: np.log1p(x))\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Create ratios between chemically relevant features\nRatios between similar features may capture interactions between molecular characteristics. For example, comparing percentages of carbon (C%) with nO (number of oxygen atoms) can highlight the molecular composition balance, which might relate to biodegradability.\n\npython\n# carbon_to_oxygen_ratio\n# Ratio between C% and nO (Number of oxygen atoms)\n# Usefulness: This ratio provides a measure of the balance between carbon and oxygen composition, which can be linked to the molecule's likelihood of being biodegradable.\ndf['carbon_to_oxygen_ratio'] = df['C%'] / (df['nO'] + 1e-6)  # Adding 1e-6 to avoid division by zero\n\n# aromatic_to_terminal_C_ratio\n# Ratio between nCb- (substituted benzene C) and nCp (terminal primary C)\n# Usefulness: Comparing substituted benzene carbons to terminal carbons could highlight structural properties associated with aromatic rings, influencing biodegradability.\ndf['aromatic_to_terminal_C_ratio'] = df['nCb-'] / (df['nCp'] + 1e-6)  # Avoid division by zero\n\n# polar_to_heavy_atom_ratio\n# Ratio between SpPosA_B(p) (polarizability index) and nHM (number of heavy atoms)\n# Usefulness: This feature captures the relationship between bond polarizability and molecular size, which may provide insight into the molecule's chemical activity and biodegradability.\ndf['polar_to_heavy_atom_ratio'] = df['SpPosA_B(p)'] / (df['nHM'] + 1e-6)  # Avoid division by zero\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Apply interaction terms for feature synthesis\nCombining features through multiplication allows the creation of new features that represent interaction effects. For example, multiplying features that describe molecular properties can help the model understand relationships that linear combinations cannot.\n\npython\n# interaction_C_percent_SubBenzeneC\n# Multiplication of C% (percentage of C atoms) and nCb- (number of substituted benzene carbons)\n# Usefulness: This interaction term emphasizes molecules with a high-carbon composition and a significant aromatic structure, which may correlate with biodegradability.\ndf['interaction_C_percent_SubBenzeneC'] = df['C%'] * df['nCb-']\n\n# interaction_SM6_L_Laplace_TI2_L\n# Multiplication of SM6_L (spectral moment of order 6 from Laplace matrix) and TI2_L (second Mohar index from Laplace matrix)\n# Usefulness: Captures higher-order interactions between molecular graph descriptors related to structural complexity and connectivity.\ndf['interaction_SM6_L_Laplace_TI2_L'] = df['SM6_L'] * df['TI2_L']\n\n# interaction_nN_nO\n# Multiplication of nN (number of nitrogen atoms) and nO (number of oxygen atoms)\n# Usefulness: This highlights molecules with significant amounts of nitrogen-oxygen combinations, such as nitro groups, which can impact biodegradability.\ndf['interaction_nN_nO'] = df['nN'] * df['nO']\nend\n\n---\n\n### Optional Step: Drop features with low variance\nSome columns, such as F01[N-N], B01[C-Br], nN-N, and N-073, have very low mean values and standard deviations, indicating almost no variability. Such features may not provide meaningful insight for the model and could instead introduce noise or redundancy.\n\npython\n# Dropping low-variance columns\n# Explanation: These features show negligible variability within the dataset, making them unlikely to contribute meaningful information to the classification task.\ndf.drop(columns=['F01[N-N]', 'B01[C-Br]', 'nN-N', 'N-073'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\nThis combination of feature transformations, interactions, and dimensionality reduction improves the feature set's relevance and quality, potentially leading to better performance in the downstream classification task of predicting chemical biodegradability.", "### Step 1: Log Transformation of Highly Skewed Features\nLogarithmic transformations can help normalize features with high skewness, improving the model's ability to detect patterns.\n\npython\n# log_F04[C-N]\n# Feature description: Logarithmic transformation of F04[C-N], the frequency of C-N bonds at topological distance 4.\n# Usefulness: Reduces skewness in the distribution of F04[C-N], making it easier for the model to interpret extreme values and improve predictive accuracy.\ndf['log_F04[C-N]'] = np.log1p(df['F04[C-N]'])\n\n# log_F03[C-O]\n# Feature description: Logarithmic transformation of F03[C-O], the frequency of C-O bonds at topological distance 3.\n# Usefulness: Normalizes the distribution of F03[C-O], reducing the impact of outliers and enhancing downstream modeling performance.\ndf['log_F03[C-O]'] = np.log1p(df['F03[C-O]'])\n\n# log_nCIR\n# Feature description: Logarithmic transformation of nCIR, the number of circuits in the molecular structure.\n# Usefulness: Reduces the large dynamic range of nCIR, helping the model better capture relationships related to structural complexity.\ndf['log_nCIR'] = np.log1p(df['nCIR'])\nend\n\n### Step 2: Create Ratios Between Chemically Relevant Features\nRatios can emphasize relationships between structural and compositional attributes.\n\npython\n# carbon_to_oxygen_ratio\n# Feature description: Ratio between C% (percentage of C atoms) and nO (number of oxygen atoms).\n# Usefulness: Highlights the balance between carbon and oxygen components in the molecule, which could play a role in biodegradability.\ndf['carbon_to_oxygen_ratio'] = df['C%'] / (df['nO'] + 1e-6)  # Adding 1e-6 to avoid division by zero\n\n# polar_to_heavy_atom_ratio\n# Feature description: Ratio between SpPosA_B(p) (polarizability index) and nHM (number of heavy atoms).\n# Usefulness: This ratio reflects the polarizability of molecular bonds in relation to molecular size, potentially impacting biodegradability.\ndf['polar_to_heavy_atom_ratio'] = df['SpPosA_B(p)'] / (df['nHM'] + 1e-6)  # Adding 1e-6 to avoid division by zero\n\n# oxygen_to_heavy_atom_ratio\n# Feature description: Ratio of nO (number of oxygen atoms) to nHM (number of heavy atoms).\n# Usefulness: Oxygen's presence relative to heavy atoms may influence the molecule's probability of being biologically degraded.\ndf['oxygen_to_heavy_atom_ratio'] = df['nO'] / (df['nHM'] + 1e-6)  # Adding 1e-6 to avoid division by zero\nend\n\n### Step 3: Interaction Terms for Capturing Relationships\nCreate new features by combining existing attributes to account for non-linear interactions.\n\npython\n# interaction_SM6_L_and_TI2_L\n# Feature description: Multiplication of SM6_L (spectral moment of order 6 from Laplace matrix) and TI2_L (second Mohar index from Laplace matrix).\n# Usefulness: Encodes higher-order interactions between molecular connectivity and structural complexity, which could be predictive of biodegradability.\ndf['interaction_SM6_L_and_TI2_L'] = df['SM6_L'] * df['TI2_L']\n\n# interaction_HyWi_B(m)_and_nO\n# Feature description: Multiplication of HyWi_B(m) (Hyper-Wiener-like index) and nO (number of oxygen atoms).\n# Usefulness: Enhances the importance of molecules with high mass-related indices and oxygen content, key factors for biodegradation prediction.\ndf['interaction_HyWi_B(m)_and_nO'] = df['HyWi_B(m)'] * df['nO']\n\n# interaction_C_percent_and_SdO\n# Feature description: Multiplication of C% (percentage of carbon atoms) and SdO (sum of dO E-states).\n# Usefulness: Encodes relationships between carbon composition and electronic state contributions, potentially indicating molecular reactivity.\ndf['interaction_C_percent_and_SdO'] = df['C%'] * df['SdO']\nend\n\n### Dropping Low-Variance Columns\nFeatures with little variation provide limited information and add noise to the model.\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column B03[C-Cl] is dropped\n# The column B03[C-Cl] is binary, with most values being zero (mean = 0.1343, std = 0.3412), offering minimal predictive value.\ndf.drop(columns=['B03[C-Cl']', inplace=True)\n\n# Explanation why the column B01[C-Br] is dropped\n# The column B01[C-Br] is highly sparse, with most values being zero (mean = 0.0442, std = 0.2058), indicating it adds little insight.\ndf.drop(columns=['B01[C-Br]'], inplace=True)\n\n# Explanation why the column nN-N is dropped\n# The column nN-N has an almost negligible mean (0.0111) and standard deviation (0.1188), showing low variability and redundancy.\ndf.drop(columns=['nN-N'], inplace=True)\nend", "Here are the three next steps for feature engineering, aiming to improve the downstream prediction task of classifying biodegradability. Each feature is designed to appropriately reflect real-world molecular interactions, enhance model interpretability, and address potential issues like skewness or lack of interaction modeling.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Log Transformation for Highly Skewed Features\nSeveral features exhibit large ranges and significant skewness. Applying a logarithmic transformation can normalize their distribution and reduce the effect of extreme values, making them more informative for machine learning models.\n\npython\n# log_F03[C-O]\n# Feature description: Logarithm of the frequency of C-O bonds at topological distance 3 (F03[C-O]).\n# Usefulness: As F03[C-O] (mean = 3.6524, median = 2.0) is positively skewed, this transformation reduces skewness and improves feature variability for the model.\ndf['log_F03[C-O]'] = np.log1p(df['F03[C-O]'])\n\n# log_F04[C-N]\n# Feature description: Logarithm of the frequency of C-N bonds at topological distance 4 (F04[C-N]).\n# Usefulness: This feature is highly skewed (mean = 0.9637, median = 0.0), and the log transformation avoids dampening outliers while making the data distribution more normal.\ndf['log_F04[C-N]'] = np.log1p(df['F04[C-N]'])\n\n# log_SdO\n# Feature description: Logarithm of the sum of dO E-states (SdO).\n# Usefulness: SdO has a wide range (0 to 71.167) and high variability. The log transformation ensures better handling of its non-linear impact.\ndf['log_SdO'] = np.log1p(df['SdO'])\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Interaction Terms for Molecular Relationships\nChemical properties often interact multiplicatively. Creating interaction terms can help the model learn relationships that are crucial for predicting biodegradability but may not be captured by individual features alone.\n\npython\n# interaction_C_percent_nO\n# Feature description: Interaction term for the percentage of carbon atoms (C%) and the number of oxygen atoms (nO).\n# Usefulness: This term highlights the combined effect of carbon and oxygen in determining biodegradability, which may reflect molecular stability or activity.\ndf['interaction_C_percent_nO'] = df['C%'] * df['nO']\n\n# interaction_nN_nO\n# Feature description: Interaction term for the number of nitrogen atoms (nN) and oxygen atoms (nO).\n# Usefulness: Nitrogen-oxygen combinations (e.g., nitro groups) are chemically significant for biodegradability, and this interaction emphasizes their joint impact.\ndf['interaction_nN_nO'] = df['nN'] * df['nO']\n\n# interaction_SM6_L_TI2_L\n# Feature description: Interaction between SM6_L (spectral moment of order 6 from Laplace matrix) and TI2_L (second Mohar index from Laplace matrix).\n# Usefulness: This captures higher-order molecular graph descriptors, representing structural complexity and connectivity that influence chemical degradability.\ndf['interaction_SM6_L_TI2_L'] = df['SM6_L'] * df['TI2_L']\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Drop Features with Low Variance\nFeatures with very low variance provide limited discriminatory power to the model and can act as noise. Removing such features helps simplify the model and improve generalizability.\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column B01[C-Br] is dropped\n# B01[C-Br] (presence/absence of C-Br at topological distance 1) has very low variance (mean = 0.0442, std = 0.2058), with most values being zero. It is unlikely to contribute to the predictive task.\ndf.drop(columns=['B01[C-Br]'], inplace=True)\n\n# Explanation why the column nN-N is dropped\n# nN-N (number of N hydrazines) has an extremely low mean (mean = 0.0111, std = 0.1188) and almost no variability. It does not provide meaningful input to the model.\ndf.drop(columns=['nN-N'], inplace=True)\n\n# Explanation why the column N-073 is dropped\n# N-073 (specific nitrogen-related structural descriptor) has a mean = 0.0300 and a standard deviation = 0.1884, with most values being zero. This low variability reduces its utility.\ndf.drop(columns=['N-073'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\nBy implementing these feature transformations (log scaling, interaction terms) and feature selection (removing low-variance features), we aim to improve the model's ability to distinguish between biodegradable and non-biodegradable chemicals. The added intuition comes from leveraging domain-specific molecular interactions and statistical transformations.", "Here are three possible next steps to improve predictions for classifying chemicals into ready biodegradable or not ready biodegradable. The steps focus on leveraging statistical transformations, meaningful interaction terms, and eliminating noise from low-variance features.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Log Transformation for Skewed Features\n\nSome features exhibit significant skewness, which can distort relationships in the dataset. Logarithmic transformation can make these features more interpretable and useful for downstream tasks.\n\npython\n# log_F03[C-O]\n# Feature description: Logarithm of the frequency of C-O bonds at topological distance 3 (F03[C-O]).\n# Usefulness: Reduces skewness in the distribution of F03[C-O] (mean = 3.6524, median = 2.0), allowing better detection of patterns that depend on chemical bonds.\ndf['log_F03[C-O]'] = np.log1p(df['F03[C-O]'])\n\n# log_SdO\n# Feature description: Logarithm of the sum of dO E-states (SdO).\n# Usefulness: Makes the wide range of SdO (range = [0.0, 71.167]) more suitable for analysis by reducing extreme outliers and transforming the data closer to normality.\ndf['log_SdO'] = np.log1p(df['SdO'])\n\n# log_nCIR\n# Feature description: Logarithm of the number of circuits (nCIR).\n# Usefulness: nCIR has a high mean (1.5245) compared to its median (1.0) and a large range (0 to 147); this transformation normalizes the data, improving its usability in downstream models.\ndf['log_nCIR'] = np.log1p(df['nCIR'])\n  \nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Create Interaction Features to Capture Relationships\n\nCreating interaction terms allows the model to learn non-linear relationships between molecular descriptors, reflecting real-world chemical interactions.\n\npython\n# interaction_C_percent_nO\n# Feature description: Interaction between the percentage of carbon atoms (C%) and the number of oxygen atoms (nO).\n# Usefulness: Reflects the combined significance of carbon and oxygen presence in molecular structures, which can influence their biodegradability.\ndf['interaction_C_percent_nO'] = df['C%'] * df['nO']\n\n# interaction_SM6_L_TI2_L\n# Feature description: Multiplication of SM6_L (spectral moment of order 6 from Laplace matrix) and TI2_L (second Mohar index from Laplace matrix).\n# Usefulness: Encodes complex interactions between molecular connectivity and structural complexity, providing insights into molecular graph properties.\ndf['interaction_SM6_L_TI2_L'] = df['SM6_L'] * df['TI2_L']\n\n# interaction_nN_HyWi_B(m)\n# Feature description: Multiplication of the number of nitrogen atoms (nN) and the Hyper-Wiener-like index from Burden matrix weighted by mass (HyWi_B(m)).\n# Usefulness: Highlights the influence of nitrogen atom count combined with the molecular size/mass on biodegradability prediction.\ndf['interaction_nN_HyWi_B(m)'] = df['nN'] * df['HyWi_B(m)']\n  \nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Drop Features with Low Variance\n\nLow-variance features likely provide minimal discriminatory power for the machine learning model, and dropping them reduces noise and improves model efficiency.\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column B01[C-Br] is dropped\n# The presence/absence of C-Br at topological distance 1 has very low variance (mean = 0.0442, std = 0.2058), and most values are zero, offering minimal predictive value.\ndf.drop(columns=['B01[C-Br]'], inplace=True)\n\n# Explanation why the column nN-N is dropped\n# The number of N hydrazines (nN-N) has a negligible mean (0.0111) and low variability (std = 0.1188), making it uninformative for the model.\ndf.drop(columns=['nN-N'], inplace=True)\n\n# Explanation why the column N-073 is dropped\n# N-073 (specific nitrogen-related structural descriptor) has a mean of 0.0300 and a standard deviation of 0.1884, indicating almost no variability. This likely adds noise to the model.\ndf.drop(columns=['N-073'], inplace=True)\n  \nend\n\n---\n\nThese steps leverage transformations to handle skewness, create deeper feature interactions for complex relationships, and streamline the feature set by eliminating noise from uninformative features. This balanced approach enhances both the interpretability and effectiveness of the downstream classification model.", "python\n# log_F03[C-O]\n# Feature description: Logarithmic transformation of F03[C-O] (frequency of C-O bonds at topological distance 3).\n# Usefulness: F03[C-O] is highly skewed (mean = 3.6524, median = 2.0, Q3 = 6.0). Applying a log transformation reduces the effect of outliers and normalizes the data, making it more suitable for downstream tasks.\ndf['log_F03[C-O]'] = np.log1p(df['F03[C-O]'])\nend\n\npython\n# carbon_to_oxygen_ratio\n# Feature description: Ratio of C% (percentage of carbon atoms) to nO (number of oxygen atoms).\n# Usefulness: This ratio highlights the balance between carbon content and oxygen atoms, which is critical for understanding the molecular structure's biodegradability. Oxygen is often associated with functional groups important for biodegradation.\ndf['carbon_to_oxygen_ratio'] = df['C%'] / (df['nO'] + 1e-6)  # Adding 1e-6 to avoid division by zero\nend\n\npython\n# interaction_SM6_L_TI2_L\n# Feature description: Multiplication of SM6_L (spectral moment of order 6 from Laplace matrix) and TI2_L (second Mohar index from Laplace matrix).\n# Usefulness: Combining these graph-based features captures molecular structural complexity and connectivity, which are key factors influencing chemical activity and biodegradability.\ndf['interaction_SM6_L_TI2_L'] = df['SM6_L'] * df['TI2_L']\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column B01[C-Br] is dropped\n# The feature B01[C-Br] (presence/absence of C-Br at topological distance 1) has very low variance (mean = 0.0442, std = 0.2058), with most values being zero. It provides limited information and adds noise to the model.\ndf.drop(columns=['B01[C-Br]'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column nN-N is dropped\n# The feature nN-N (number of N hydrazines) has extremely low mean (0.0111) and standard deviation (0.1188), indicating almost no variability. Its lack of significant information makes it unhelpful for the predictive task.\ndf.drop(columns=['nN-N'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column N-073 is dropped\n# The feature N-073 (specific nitrogen-related structure) exhibits negligible variability (mean = 0.0300, std = 0.1884), with most values being zero. Such sparse features are unlikely to contribute meaningfully to the model.\ndf.drop(columns=['N-073'], inplace=True)\nend", "### Next Three Steps for Feature Engineering\n\nHere are three simple, intuitive, and effective steps to improve the prediction of chemical biodegradability, balancing statistical transformations, feature interactions, and feature selection.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Logarithmic Transformation for Skewed Features\n\nSeveral features exhibit significant skewness, which can distort their relationships with the target variable. Applying a logarithmic transformation reduces the impact of extreme values and normalizes the distributions.\n\npython\n# log_F03[C-O]\n# Feature description: Logarithm of the frequency of C-O bonds at topological distance 3 (F03[C-O]).\n# Usefulness: F03[C-O] is highly skewed (mean = 3.6524, median = 2.0, Q3 = 6.0). A log transformation reduces skewness and better represents chemical properties in a way the model can learn.\ndf['log_F03[C-O]'] = np.log1p(df['F03[C-O]'])\n\n# log_SdO\n# Feature description: Logarithm of the sum of dO E-states (SdO).\n# Usefulness: SdO has a wide range (0.0 to 71.167) and high variability. The logarithmic transformation normalizes the distribution while maintaining its relative influence.\ndf['log_SdO'] = np.log1p(df['SdO'])\n\n# log_nCIR\n# Feature description: Logarithm of the number of circuits (nCIR) in the molecular structure.\n# Usefulness: nCIR has a large range (0 to 147) and a high mean (1.5245) relative to its median. Applying a log transformation normalizes it and reduces the impact of extreme circuit counts.\ndf['log_nCIR'] = np.log1p(df['nCIR'])\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Create Interaction Terms to Model Relationships\n\nCertain features may interact multiplicatively to influence biodegradability. Creating such interaction terms allows the model to better capture the underlying relationships.\n\npython\n# interaction_C_percent_nO\n# Feature description: Interaction between the percentage of carbon atoms (C%) and the number of oxygen atoms (nO).\n# Usefulness: Highlights the combined chemical significance of carbon and oxygen content in structures, which may influence biodegradability.\ndf['interaction_C_percent_nO'] = df['C%'] * df['nO']\n\n# interaction_SM6_L_TI2_L\n# Feature description: Multiplication of SM6_L (spectral moment of order 6 from Laplace matrix) and TI2_L (second Mohar index from Laplace matrix).\n# Usefulness: Encodes relationships between molecular complexity and graph-based structure, aiding the model in learning from higher-order molecular descriptors.\ndf['interaction_SM6_L_TI2_L'] = df['SM6_L'] * df['TI2_L']\n\n# interaction_nO_nN\n# Feature description: Interaction term for the number of oxygen atoms (nO) and nitrogen atoms (nN).\n# Usefulness: Chemical combinations of oxygen and nitrogen (e.g., nitro or amine groups) are often indicative of biodegradability. This term emphasizes their joint impact.\ndf['interaction_nO_nN'] = df['nO'] * df['nN']\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Drop Columns with Low Variance\n\nFeatures with extremely low variance provide minimal information for distinguishing between classes and can be removed to reduce noise.\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column B01[C-Br] is dropped\n# B01[C-Br] (presence/absence of C-Br at topological distance 1) has very low variance (mean = 0.0442, std = 0.2058). Most values are zero, so it adds little predictive value.\ndf.drop(columns=['B01[C-Br]'], inplace=True)\n\n# Explanation why the column nN-N is dropped\n# nN-N (number of N hydrazines) has an extremely low mean (0.0111) and standard deviation (0.1188). This feature lacks meaningful variation and is uninformative.\ndf.drop(columns=['nN-N'], inplace=True)\n\n# Explanation why the column N-073 is dropped\n# N-073 (a specific nitrogen-related descriptor) shows very little variability (mean = 0.0300, std = 0.1884) and is sparse, making it unlikely to contribute to the predictive task.\ndf.drop(columns=['N-073'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\nThese steps ensure better feature representation while reducing noise, leading to improved model performance on the task of classifying chemical biodegradability.", "python\n# Feature 1: log_F03[C-O]\n# Feature description: Logarithm of the frequency of C-O bonds at topological distance 3 (F03[C-O]).\n# Usefulness: F03[C-O] is highly skewed (mean = 3.6524, median = 2.0, Q3 = 6.0). Log transformation normalizes the feature, reducing the impact of outliers and making the data more suitable for downstream tasks, particularly for models sensitive to skewed distributions.\ndf['log_F03[C-O]'] = np.log1p(df['F03[C-O]'])\nend\n\npython\n# Feature 2: carbon_to_oxygen_ratio\n# Feature description: Ratio of C% (percentage of carbon atoms) to nO (number of oxygen atoms).\n# Usefulness: This ratio captures the balance between carbon content and oxygen atoms, which may influence chemical biodegradability. A higher ratio could suggest a lack of oxygen-containing functional groups, essential for biodegradation, while a lower ratio indicates more oxygen in the molecular structure.\ndf['carbon_to_oxygen_ratio'] = df['C%'] / (df['nO'] + 1e-6)  # Adding 1e-6 to avoid division by zero\nend\n\npython\n# Feature 3: interaction_SM6_L_TI2_L\n# Feature description: Multiplication of SM6_L (spectral moment of order 6 from Laplace matrix) and TI2_L (second Mohar index from Laplace matrix).\n# Usefulness: This interaction term combines graph-based structural features, capturing molecular complexity and connectivity patterns, which are significant for distinguishing between biodegradable and non-biodegradable chemicals.\ndf['interaction_SM6_L_TI2_L'] = df['SM6_L'] * df['TI2_L']\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column B01[C-Br] is dropped\n# The feature B01[C-Br] (presence/absence of C-Br at topological distance 1) has very low variance (mean = 0.0442, std = 0.2058), with most values being zero. Its limited variability diminishes its predictive power and adds unnecessary noise to the model.\ndf.drop(columns=['B01[C-Br]'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column nN-N is dropped\n# The feature nN-N (number of N hydrazines) has an extremely low mean (0.0111) and standard deviation (0.1188), signifying almost no variability in the data. As a consequence, it is unlikely to contribute meaningful information for classification.\ndf.drop(columns=['nN-N'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column N-073 is dropped\n# The feature N-073 (specific nitrogen-related structure) has a negligible mean (0.0300) with a low standard deviation (0.1884), indicating that the feature is sparse and provides minimal information useful for prediction tasks.\ndf.drop(columns=['N-073'], inplace=True)\nend"]###,
